WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.507
Some stories begin with a handle because the old name no longer fits the person walking into the shadows.

2
00:00:07.507 --> 00:00:15.014
The Name She Chose is an Origin Dossier backing of tone: a character history shaped into playable memory, not a generic biography pasted behind the sheet.

3
00:00:15.014 --> 00:00:22.521
The past arrives as pressure. A contact with a reason to call. A debt that was never fully paid.

4
00:00:22.521 --> 00:00:30.028
A boundary the runner refuses to cross again. A name that sounds simple until the table learns what it cost.

5
00:00:30.028 --> 00:00:37.535
This is what campaign memory should feel like when it is handled carefully: approved by the player, useful to the GM, dramatic without stealing authorship.

6
00:00:37.535 --> 00:00:45.042
The dossier can turn biography into hooks, images, scene packets, and narration, but it does not silently change the build.

7
00:00:45.042 --> 00:00:52.549
Ware, money, qualities, magic, legality, and rules remain in the sheet where the table can inspect them.

8
00:00:52.549 --> 00:01:00.056
When the GM needs a thread, the origin can answer with emotional weight.

9
00:01:00.056 --> 00:01:07.563
When the player needs a reason, the past can speak without becoming a lecture.

10
00:01:07.563 --> 00:01:15.070
A strong dossier gives the table handles without turning the runner into a fixed script. It invites play, complication, and choice.

11
00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:22.577
It also gives media a standard: evocative enough to matter, bounded enough to reject, and clear enough that the player remains the author of the person at the center.

12
00:01:22.577 --> 00:01:30.084
The sheet says what she can do. The dossier helps the table understand why she chose to become this person now.
